  <section id="vidy">
    <h2>Types of tumours</h2>
    <p>A tumour is any growth of tissue that should not be there. More than a hundred types are known in and around the brain. For a patient two questions matter most: where the tumour grows from and how aggressive it is.</p>
    <h3>By origin</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Primary</strong> — grow from tissues of the head itself: from the brain coverings (<a href="/en/treatment/tumors/meningioma/">meningioma</a>), from brain cells (<a href="/en/treatment/tumors/glioma/">glioma</a>), from the pituitary (<a href="/en/treatment/tumors/pituitary-adenoma/">adenoma</a>), from nerve sheaths (<a href="/en/treatment/tumors/schwannoma/">schwannoma</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>Secondary</strong> — <a href="/en/treatment/tumors/brain-metastases/">metastases</a> of tumours from other organs that reached the brain with the blood.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>By growth pattern</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Benign</strong> tumours grow slowly, have a clear border and push the brain aside rather than invading it. They can often be removed completely.</li>
      <li><strong>Malignant</strong> tumours grow faster and invade surrounding tissue. Removal is the first step; radiotherapy and chemotherapy are usually needed afterwards.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>But "benign" in the brain does not mean "safe". Even such a tumour can press on the brain, nerves and vessels, because there is no spare room in the skull.</p>
    <Scheme />
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <p>A tumour may stay silent for a long time: the brain adapts to slow growth. Watch for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>headache that is worse in the morning, with nausea or vomiting;</li>
      <li>first-ever seizures — especially in an adult;</li>
      <li>increasing weakness or numbness of an arm, leg or half of the face;</li>
      <li>problems with speech or vision, double vision;</li>
      <li>changes in behaviour and memory noticed by family;</li>
      <li>hormonal problems: menstrual changes, discharge from the breast, changes in appearance (with pituitary tumours).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These symptoms do not necessarily mean a tumour, but they should not be ignored: see a neurologist. <a href={`${tg}/141`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Headache red flags that should not wait</a> are on my Telegram channel (in Russian).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="nashli">
    <h2>If a tumour is found on MRI</h2>
    <p>More and more often a tumour is an incidental finding — the MRI was done for another reason. <a href={`${tg}/236`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Here is one such case</a> (in Russian).</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Do not panic, but do not postpone.</strong> An MRI report is not yet a diagnosis.</li>
      <li><strong>Have an MRI with contrast</strong> if you have not had one: many tumours are poorly seen without contrast.</li>
      <li><strong>Show the images to a neurosurgeon</strong> — not just the report, but the images themselves on a disc or via a link. They show whether surgery is needed, how urgent it is and which tests are still required.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Observation</strong> — for small benign tumours without symptoms: follow-up MRI on the schedule set by the doctor.</li>
      <li><strong>Surgery</strong> — the main method for most tumours that grow, cause symptoms or look suspicious for malignancy. It removes the tumour and provides tissue for histology.</li>
      <li><strong>Radiotherapy and radiosurgery</strong> — after surgery for malignant tumours, or instead of it when a tumour is small and hard to reach.</li>
      <li><strong>Chemotherapy and drug treatment</strong> — prescribed by an oncologist based on histology. Some pituitary tumours are treated with tablets.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For malignant tumours the strategy is discussed at a tumour board — a neurosurgeon, an oncologist and a radiation oncologist together.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="operaciya">
    <h2>How surgery is done</h2>
    <p>I remove most tumours microsurgically, under the microscope. Before surgery the MRI is loaded into the <strong>neuronavigation</strong> system: it matches the images to the patient's head in real time. I point a probe and see where the tumour border is, where healthy tissue is and where not to go. This allows a smaller approach and a more precise, safer removal. This is how we removed <a href={`${tg}/96`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">a large temporal lobe tumour</a> (in Russian).</p>
    <p>Some skull base and pituitary tumours are removed <strong>endoscopically through the nose</strong> — without an incision on the head. More in the article <a href="/en/treatment/tumors/pituitary-adenoma/">"Pituitary adenoma"</a>.</p>
    <p>After surgery the tumour tissue is sent for <strong>histology</strong>. Its result determines whether further treatment is needed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="srochno" slot="urgent">
    <h2>When to seek urgent help</h2>
    <div class="callout alert">
      <p>First-ever seizures, a sudden severe headache, rapidly increasing weakness of an arm or leg, speech problems, confusion or drowsiness — <strong>call emergency services immediately (112; 103 in Russia).</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Article>
